reflection:36 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:2 ;
    schema:name "Acclimatizing to danger" ;
    schema:text "Lewis notes his section's nerves have already adapted to combat's minor dangers, in sharp contrast to green American HQ troops fresh from peaceful Kansas and Wisconsin, whose raw panic he finds more dangerous than the enemy."@en .

reflection:37 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:6 ;
    schema:name "Official history vs. what he witnessed" ;
    schema:text "After the chaotic Salerno counterattack, Lewis predicts official history will dress up the retreat with false dignity, when what he actually saw was ineptitude and cowardice spreading down from the command."@en .

reflection:38 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:15 ;
    schema:name "The psychology of informants" ;
    schema:text "Lewis compares an informant to a duckling imprinting on the first sympathetic listener: driven by a strange, exclusive loyalty to one master rather than by payment, and never transferring allegiance once given."@en .

reflection:39 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:20 ;
    schema:name "Educated poverty and family pride" ;
    schema:text "Reflecting on Lattarullo, Lewis notes Naples has thousands of unemployed lawyers and doctors — families starve themselves so a son can be addressed with the honorific avvocato or dottore, even if he never practises."@en .

reflection:40 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:23 ;
    schema:name "Naples seen from above — un-European, oriental" ;
    schema:text "Awaiting a rumoured mass mine detonation from the heights of the Vomero, Lewis is struck, seeing the city whole for the first time, by how un-European and almost oriental Naples appears from a distance."@en .

reflection:41 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:24 ;
    schema:name "Generals passing moral responsibility" ;
    schema:text "Asked to help decide whether to execute a captured teenage German soldier, Lewis observes that generals who like to appear ruthless are often eager in practice to offload the moral responsibility for such decisions onto subordinates like himself."@en .

reflection:42 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:27 ;
    schema:name "Conversion to pessimism — the blind orphan girls" ;
    schema:text "Watching a group of weeping blind orphan girls ignored by indifferent diners in a restaurant, Lewis says the experience changed his outlook permanently: he had believed people eventually come to terms with pain, and now understands he was wrong."@en .

reflection:43 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:28 ;
    schema:name "Genteel poverty as a historical constant" ;
    schema:text "Comparing his near-destitute contacts Lattarullo, Losurdo and Mosca to Alexandre Dumas's 1835 account of Naples's aristocracy, Lewis concludes their poverty isn't just a wartime effect but a century-old pattern of the city's oversupplied professional classes."@en .

reflection:44 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:34 ;
    schema:name "Naples's unconscious class proximity" ;
    schema:text "Lewis observes that in his neighbourhood the rich and poor live physically side by side — grand palazzi and one-room working-class households sharing the same buildings — while remaining almost entirely unconscious of one another."@en .

reflection:45 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:37 ;
    schema:name "Omertà and the irony of 'liberation'" ;
    schema:text "After a wire-cutting investigation dead-ends in silence, a Carabinieri sergeant sardonically thanks Lewis for teaching Naples 'what democratic justice is all about' — Lewis registers the biting irony without the sergeant cracking a smile."@en .

reflection:46 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:38 ;
    schema:name "The historical roots of the Zona di Camorra's poverty" ;
    schema:text "Taking charge of security in the Camorra-controlled towns north of Naples, Lewis notes the great fertility of the land was extracted for centuries to build Naples's ducal palaces, leaving the peasants who worked it in conditions close to slavery."@en .

reflection:47 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:45 ;
    schema:name "Naples's cultural distance from its own hinterland" ;
    schema:text "Lewis observes that cosmopolitan Naples knows and cares nothing for the archaic, vendetta-bound countryside just miles away — his contact Lattarullo has visited Rome many times but never once turned aside into nearby Afragola."@en .

reflection:48 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:46 ;
    schema:name "Historical amnesia of the ordinary Neapolitan" ;
    schema:text "Watching street storytellers recount the deeds of Charlemagne, Lewis reflects that ten centuries of invading armies and drowned revolutions have left no mark on the common Neapolitan's imagination or memory."@en .

reflection:49 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:50 ;
    schema:name "Justice only for the poor" ;
    schema:text "Lewis observes that the crackdown on the black market only ever catches those with no one to speak for them and no money to bribe their way out, while the well-connected are acquitted or let off with light sentences."@en .

reflection:50 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:52 ;
    schema:name "Bombing kills only the poor — Allied and German alike" ;
    schema:text "After a bad air raid kills children in Santa Lucia, Lewis notes bitterly that both the Germans and the Allies, in these indiscriminate raids, only ever seem to kill the poor."@en .

reflection:51 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:54 ;
    schema:name "Faith in permanence against Vesuvius's certainty" ;
    schema:text "Visiting San Sebastiano as lava approaches, Lewis marvels that its people build to last centuries and refuse to admit the town's eventual destruction is a mathematical certainty — civic permanency treated as an article of religious faith."@en .

reflection:52 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:56 ;
    schema:name "Miracle fervor as collective coping with crisis" ;
    schema:text "Lewis observes that wartime hardship has pushed Naples back into the Middle Ages, with a sudden explosion of talking, bleeding, and weeping religious images answering a citywide craving for miracles and cures."@en .

reflection:53 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:59 ;
    schema:name "Capri as escapist psychological release" ;
    schema:text "On a boat trip to Capri, Lewis compares the island to hashish — bringing out whatever demon lurks at the bottom of the personality — and finds it a world entirely disengaged from the war, unlike Naples."@en .

reflection:54 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:61 ;
    schema:name "Economic desperation behind 'prostitution'" ;
    schema:text "Investigating marriage candidates, Lewis finds most so-called prostitutes are ordinary girls with no other means of survival, and reflects that with the whole population out of work and producing nothing, it's remarkable they can even find a man to support them at all."@en .

reflection:55 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:62 ;
    schema:name "Solidarity of the poor toward escaped POWs" ;
    schema:text "Interviewing escaped British POWs, Lewis notes they'd been trained to seek help only from the poor, who 'have nothing to lose' — and reflects that in fact the poor risked everything, since sheltering escapees was a capital risk."@en .

reflection:56 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:64 ;
    schema:name "The arithmetic of survival wages" ;
    schema:text "Cross-checking a marriage candidate's income against real wage figures — a major earning £7 10s a month, a postman far less — Lewis lays out the impossible arithmetic facing a family of seven trying to survive on about a pound a month."@en .

reflection:57 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:71 ;
    schema:name "Injustice of the anti-bandit crackdown" ;
    schema:text "Lewis opens by calling the General's mass-arrest campaign against bandits 'a shameful example of the perfidiousness and injustices of this war we conduct behind the scenes' — the crackdown falls only on small-time opportunists, not real gang leadership."@en .

reflection:58 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:73 ;
    schema:name "Justice bought — the big fish always escape" ;
    schema:text "Visiting the imperturbable racketeer Fortuna in prison, Lewis reflects that every 'big fish' defendant in the corruption cases has gone free through disappearing witnesses, lost documents, and bribed officials, while small offenders like old Antonio Priore serve long sentences."@en .

reflection:59 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:75 ;
    schema:name "Postwar political fragmentation and 'respectable insanity'" ;
    schema:text "Surveying Italy's sudden explosion of some sixty political parties, some proposing perpetual-motion machines as national policy, Lewis remarks that this is a season and situation in which insanity has become almost respectable."@en .

reflection:60 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:76 ;
    schema:name "Selective reporting of Allied troops' crimes" ;
    schema:text "After describing the vigilante killing of five Moroccan soldiers in reprisal for earlier atrocities, Lewis wonders drily whether any account of the episode will make it into the Allied Psychological Warfare Bureau's bulletin."@en .

reflection:61 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:80 ;
    schema:name "Pozzuoli's separateness from Naples" ;
    schema:text "Visiting as a tourist, Lewis finds Pozzuoli calm, self-absorbed, and almost untouched by the war — noting it lies outside the Zona di Camorra and its 'secret tribal life', with a population distinct from Neapolitans in dialect, custom, and possibly blood."@en .

reflection:62 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:81 ;
    schema:name "Naples as an 'Eastern city'" ;
    schema:text "Quoting the journalist Scarfoglio's line that Naples is 'the only Eastern city where there is no residential European quarter,' Lewis finds it still holds true, prompted by an aristocrat's theatrically staged deathbed farewell."@en .

reflection:63 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:89 ;
    schema:name "Fascism remembered as a 'golden interlude'" ;
    schema:text "Discussing Southern Italy's long history of banditry with the schoolmaster Don Ubaldo, Lewis records that most people were already beginning to see the Fascist era as a golden interlude of security and firm government."@en .

reflection:64 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:92 ;
    schema:name "Becoming drawn into the system" ;
    schema:text "After quietly watching a black-market pharmacy relabel stolen drugs to help a sick friend, Lewis catches himself: 'I am gradually becoming drawn into the system!' — a rare moment of self-aware complicity."@en .

reflection:65 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:97 ;
    schema:name "Collective omertà — the town that saw nothing" ;
    schema:text "Investigating a reported shotgun murder that leaves no trace but blood on the cobbles, Lewis finds everyone he asks — waiters, shopkeepers, even the Marshal — trained to silence, bland smiles, and seeing nothing."@en .

reflection:66 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:100 ;
    schema:name "Gift-exchange vs. bribery" ;
    schema:text "Lewis reconsiders the routine 100-lire notes offered for travel passes: not bribes in the Western sense, but what he compares to an African tribal system of ritual gift-giving ('dashes') that a well-bred person is expected to honour. By refusing them out of principle, he concludes he has unintentionally offended and lost the trust of people around him."@en .

reflection:67 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:101 ;
    schema:name "Liberation's disappointing aftermath vs. Fascist-era stability" ;
    schema:text "Lewis reflects that a year after the Allies 'liberated' Naples from Fascism, the population remains hungrier, more disease-ridden, and more lawless than before, with only a distant and compromised promise of corrupt democracy to show for it — concluding that the Mussolini years must now seem like a lost paradise by comparison."@en .

reflection:68 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:102 ;
    schema:name "How Southern Italy transforms outsiders" ;
    schema:text "Reunited with an old colleague after eighteen months apart, Lewis is struck by how completely Southern Italy has transformed the Englishman — he would now take him for an Italian in a British uniform."@en .

reflection:69 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:103 ;
    schema:name "The addictive pull of intelligence work" ;
    schema:text "Lewis attributes his declining health partly to overwork, describing the strange excitement of 'legalised eavesdropping on humanity' as a fascination that keeps him working up to fifteen-hour days — to the point of falling asleep at the wheel on three recent occasions."@en .

reflection:70 a schema:CreativeWork ;
    schema:isPartOf entry:108 ;
    schema:name "Abrupt departure — no time for goodbyes" ;
    schema:text "Suddenly ordered to leave for a new posting, Lewis reflects with wistful regret on all the small rituals of Naples life he will have no time to close out — one last marsala with a contact, one last view from the Vomero, one last meal at Zi' Teresa's."@en .

folklore:blessed-egideo a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A holy figure whose preserved body is venerated at the church of San Pasquale in Naples."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The incorrupt body Lewis views in its two-hundred-year-old glass case — serene 'to the point of indifference' — locally famous as the protector of pregnant women, and two miracles short of full sainthood."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Blessed Egideo" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/blessed-egideo/> .

folklore:droit-du-seigneur-sagranella a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "The lord's claimed right of sexual access to the women of his estate."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Said, at Sagranella, to be still practised as a matter of course on the neighbouring big estate, whose steward reportedly feels a prospective woman worker's muscles before hiring her for a sixteen-hour day."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Droit du seigneur (Sagranella)" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/droit-du-seigneur-sagranella/> .

folklore:fertility-offering-superstition a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A gesture read as a survival of ancient fertility worship."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "A woman's ritual gesture that Lewis takes for an offering to a fertility deity — one of the glimpses of pre-Christian cult he keeps catching in and around Naples."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "fertility offering superstition" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/fertility-offering-superstition/> .

folklore:festival-of-the-lilies-nola a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "Paulinus of Nola born Pontius Meropius Anicius Paulinus, was a Roman poet, writer, senator, and bishop. He attained the ranks of suffect consul and governor of Campania, but, prompted by the counsels of his Hispanic wife Therasia of Nola and by the assassination of the emperor Gratian, abandoned his career and was baptized as a Christian. Later, probably after Therasia's death, he became bishop of Nola in Campania."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Nola's Feast of St Paulinus, in which towering wooden 'lily' structures are carried through the streets; in 1944 the procession brought the town to a standstill and sliced through the army's telephone cables by the hundred — Eric Williams's despair."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Festival of the Lilies (Nola)" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Paulinus_of_Nola> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/festival-of-the-lilies-nola/> .

folklore:flying-monk-of-pomigliano a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A stigmatic monk of Pomigliano credited with the power of flight — identified in the book's own footnote as the future Padre Pio."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "He claims to have flown through the air to rescue a downed pilot, and displays the stigmata — one of the wave of miracles sweeping the region in 1944."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "flying monk of Pomigliano" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/flying-monk-of-pomigliano/> .

folklore:guardia-sanframondi-flagellant-rite a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "Guardia Sanframondi is a town and comune in the Province of Benevento, in Campania region, in Italy. It is best known for its wine production, the wine festival Vinalia and for its Christian penitential rite held every seven years."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The seven-yearly penitential procession in honour of the Virgin, revived in 1944 — described to Lewis by a colleague: robes soaked with blood, and one banner-carrier nearly lynched after being publicly named a cuckold."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Guardia Sanframondi flagellant rite" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Guardia_Sanframondi> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/guardia-sanframondi-flagellant-rite/> .

folklore:hunchbacks-as-luck-bringers a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A Neapolitan belief that a hunched back brings good fortune."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Lewis notes lottery-ticket buyers touching or stroking a hunchback's hump as part of the purchase."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Hunchbacks as luck-bringers" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/hunchbacks-as-luck-bringers/> .

folklore:ischia-radioactive-cure-belief a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A local conviction that Ischia's natural radioactivity is health-giving."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The belief that the Naples-facing side of Ischia is beneficially radioactive — slimming, good for the kidneys, bladder and complexion — is why Lola and Susanna go there for the summer 'to deflate'."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Ischia radioactive cure belief" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/ischia-radioactive-cure-belief/> .

folklore:madonna-of-pompeii a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "The Pontifical Shrine of the Blessed Virgin of the Rosary of Pompei is a Catholic cathedral, Marian pontifical shrine, and Pontifical minor basilica commissioned and co-founded by Bartolo Longo and his wife the Countess Mariana di Fusco, located in Pompei, Italy. It is the see of the Territorial Prelature of Pompei."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "A soldier gives Lewis a scrap of tinsel torn from her mantle, promising it will render him bulletproof for a year."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Madonna of Pompeii" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shrine_of_the_Virgin_of_the_Rosary_of_Pompei> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/madonna-of-pompeii/> .

folklore:parenti-di-san-gennaro a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A clique of aged Neapolitan women held to be the blood descendants of San Gennaro."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Led in to take their places around the cathedral altar, they are credited with the power — and the duty — of browbeating their ancestor into performing the blood miracle."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Parenti di San Gennaro" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/parenti-di-san-gennaro/> .

folklore:pazzariello a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A traditional Neapolitan costumed figure, part street-advertiser, part exorcist; 'one is born a pazzariello, not made one'."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Cocked hat, stick, drummers and piper, hired to caper into a newly reopening shop — explained as advertising for the illiterate, but 'his real function is that of an exorcist', driving out evil spirits and the evil eye. Lewis sees his first on the Via San Pasquale."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "pazzariello" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/pazzariello/> .

folklore:professional-wailers a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "Mourners paid to weep and lament at a funeral, amplifying the family's display of loss."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Lewis notes 'several professional wailers in attendance' at the funeral of a Carabiniere murdered by bandits at Casoria."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Professional wailers" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/professional-wailers/> .

folklore:sagranella-fox-cult a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description """While stationed in southern Italy during the war, Lewis came across accounts of "fox-magic" in Sagranella, a remote village located in the hills behind Benevento. \r
\r
He noted that the village felt so isolated and archaic that it "seems hardly to have moved out of the Bronze Age."According to his writings, the local cult ritual involved two primary acts:\r
\r
- The Ritual Sacrifice: Every year, the villagers would capture a live fox and burn it to death.\r
- The Display: After the burning, the fox's tail was severed and hung like a banner from a pole erected at the entrance of the village.\r
\r
Historians and travel writers often link this specific practice to ancient Samnite or localized pagan traditions in the region, where animals were sacrificed to ward off evil spirits, ensure agricultural fertility, or mark territory."""@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The annual ritual of the hill village where the section is billeted after Don Ubaldo's death: a live fox burned to death and its tail hung 'like a banner' from a pole at the village entrance, near an Easter Island-style stone head probably older than the Samnites."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Sagranella fox-cult" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/sagranella-fox-cult/> .

folklore:san-gaetano a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "Gaetano dei Conti di Thiene, known in English as Saint Cajetan, was an Italian Catholic priest and religious reformer, co-founder of the Theatines. He is recognised as a saint in the Catholic Church, and his feast day is 7 August."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Invoked as a 'powerful exorcist': the remedy for a Via Carducci block of flats blighted by suicides would be for the neighbours to club together and build a street shrine under his protection."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "San Gaetano" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Cajetan> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/san-gaetano/> .

folklore:san-rocco a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "Roch, also called Rock in English, was a Majorcan Catholic confessor whose death is commemorated on 16 August and 9 September in Italy; he was especially invoked against the plague."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Advising the exhausted Captain Frazer, Lewis recommends a medal of San Rocco — 'patron of coitus reservatus' — to be had in any religious-supplies shop."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "San Rocco" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Roch> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/san-rocco/> .

folklore:san-sebastiano a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "Sebastian was an early Christian saint and martyr. According to traditional belief, he was killed during the Diocletianic Persecution of Christians. He was initially tied to a post or tree and shot with arrows, though this did not kill him."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The patron of the town in the lava's path; his processional image is carried out to confront the 1944 eruption, and the smuggling-in of a rival San Gennaro image has to be hidden under a sheet to avoid giving him offence."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "San Sebastiano" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Saint_Sebastian> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/san-sebastiano/> .

folklore:santa-maria-del-carmine a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "Santa Maria del Carmine is a church in Naples, Italy. It is at one end of Piazza Mercato, the centre of civic life in Naples for many centuries until it was cut off from the rest of the city by urban renewal in 1900. The church was founded in the 13th century by Carmelite friars driven from the Holy Land in the Crusades, presumably arriving in the Bay of Naples aboard Amalfitan ships."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Her image, first said to have bowed its head to dodge a cannon-shot during Alfonso of Aragon's siege of Naples, now 'does this as a matter of daily routine' in the 1944 wave of miracles."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Santa Maria del Carmine" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Santa_Maria_del_Carmine,_Naples> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/santa-maria-del-carmine/> .

folklore:santantonio-abate a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "Anthony the Great was a Christian monk from Egypt, revered since his death as a saint. He is distinguished from other saints named Anthony, such as Anthony of Padua, by various epithets: Anthony of Egypt, Anthony the Abbot, Anthony of the Desert, Anthony the Anchorite, Anthony the Hermit, and Anthony of Thebes. For his importance among the Desert Fathers and to all later Christian monasticism, he is also known as the Father of All Monks."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "During the eruption he briefly displaces San Gennaro as the saint charged with holding back Vesuvius's fire."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Sant'Antonio Abate" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anthony_the_Great> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/santantonio-abate/> .

folklore:santaspreno a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "Aspren or Asprenas was a 1st-century Christian saint and venerated as the first Bishop of Naples."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The patron invoked against headaches: sufferers push their heads through a hole in the wall of his chapel in search of relief — one of the cures Lewis lists in the miracle-fever of spring 1944."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Sant'Aspreno" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aspren> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/santaspreno/> .

folklore:santo-agnello a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A Naples church, site of a 'speaking crucifix' during the 1944 miracle fervour."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Its crucifix carries on a running dialogue with the image of Santa Maria d'Intercessione — reported daily in the newspapers of spring 1944."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Santo Agnello" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/santo-agnello/> .

folklore:st-andrews-manna-amalfi a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "Amalfi Cathedral is a medieval Roman Catholic cathedral in the Piazza del Duomo, Amalfi, Italy. It is dedicated to the Apostle Saint Andrew whose relics are kept here. Formerly the archiepiscopal seat of the Diocese of Amalfi, it has been since 1986 that of the Diocese of Amalfi-Cava de' Tirreni."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The relic in the crypt of Amalfi Cathedral said to exude a miraculous liquid several times a year; Del Giudice asks Lewis to drive him there, and queues to buy a sample."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "St Andrew's Manna (Amalfi)" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amalfi_Cathedral> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/st-andrews-manna-amalfi/> .

folklore:st-john-san-giovanni-a-carbonara a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A Naples church holding a blood relic said to liquefy."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "One of the churches whose blood relic is reported to be bubbling amid the spring 1944 miracle fever."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "St John / San Giovanni a Carbonara" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/st-john-san-giovanni-a-carbonara/> .

folklore:st-michael a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "The Sanctuary of Saint Michael the Archangel is a Roman Catholic shrine on Mount Gargano, Italy, part of the commune of Monte Sant'Angelo, in the province of Foggia, northern Apulia. It has the dignity of a minor basilica."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The cave-sanctuary at Monte Sant'Angelo in Puglia — source of the venerated lump of rock that is, with a royal salver, the one treasured possession in Lattarullo's bare flat."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "St Michael" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanctuary_of_Monte_Sant%27Angelo> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/st-michael/> .

folklore:stork-veneration a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A Southern Italian taboo against harming storks, held to bring bad luck."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "A village that ate its storks' young during the famine is regarded as having called down calamity on itself."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Stork veneration" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/stork-veneration/> .

folklore:vendetta-mourning-ritual a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A funeral rite of the Southern Italian interior in which the dead man's mother mouths his wounds as a pledge of revenge."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "When a man is killed in a vendetta his mother, if living, kisses and sucks at his wounds at the funeral — the kind of 'Bronze-Age' custom Lattarullo, ten miles away in Naples, knows nothing of."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Vendetta mourning ritual" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/vendetta-mourning-ritual/> .

folklore:visionary-child-local-bernadette a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A child visionary of the Campi Flegrei reporting Marian apparitions in spring 1944."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "A twelve-year-old girl to whom the Virgin is said to appear with 'comforting messages for the population'; crowds flock out to watch, and the mayor of Marano arrives in a motor-hearse for want of other transport."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "visionary child (local 'Bernadette')" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/visionary-child-local-bernadette/> .

folklore:zio-di-roma a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A hired mourner who impersonates a distinguished 'uncle from Rome' at a funeral to raise the bereaved family's standing."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Lattarullo's suspended sideline, resumed once Rome falls — complete with a rented car and driver and a special 'Zio di Roma' suit."@en ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:folklore ;
    schema:name "Zio di Roma" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/folklore/zio-di-roma/> .

person:albano a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Camorrista / bandit" ;
    schema:description "The partisan leader of Torrito, a village near Aversa."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Lewis reluctantly travels to Torrito to hear his account of the 'heroic four days' of the September 1943 uprising and the SS reprisal that followed — a massacre that has left the whole village in mourning. He finds Albano a haggard, haunted man who speaks very quietly, as if afraid of being overheard."@en ;
    schema:name "Albano" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/albano/> .

person:alberto-gemelli a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Civilian friend" ;
    schema:description "A Naples neighbour of the arrested Signora Esposito-Lau, and Norah Gemelli's husband."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Part of the circle of friends Lewis builds after that arrest and hopes will outlast the war; when a true leak about the Anzio landing is traced to a dinner at the Gemellis', Lewis is ordered back to pump his own friends for more."@en ;
    schema:name "Alberto Gemelli" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/alberto-gemelli/> .

person:avvocato-mosca a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Informant / contact" ;
    schema:description "An impoverished Naples lawyer, entitled to style himself Conte but no longer bothering."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "One of the trio of genteel-poor bachelor contacts — with Losurdo and Lattarullo — whose grand, mouldering, half-empty palazzi on the Via Chiaia become Lewis's picture of the ruined Neapolitan gentry."@en ;
    schema:name "Avvocato Mosca" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/avvocato-mosca/> .

person:contessa-della-peruta a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Aristocrat" ;
    schema:description "A beautiful, refined aristocrat living in a near-castle above Santa Maria della Colombina."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The subject of an 'extraordinary' marriage vetting: the elegance turns out to be staged with borrowed furniture in an otherwise empty house — a sharp variation on the genteel poverty Lewis keeps meeting."@en ;
    schema:name "Contessa della Peruta" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/contessa-della-peruta/> .

person:cuoca a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Camorrista / bandit" ;
    schema:description "The last of the great Camorra chieftains, a figure of the 1920s."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "His end is told on Lewis's tourist day at Pozzuoli: deposed by his own underlings, he was feasted and embraced at a 'funeral feast' at Vicenzo a Mare, then taken away and stabbed to death."@en ;
    schema:name "Cuoca" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/cuoca/> .

person:de-micco a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Camorrista / bandit" ;
    schema:description "A farmer in the Benevento countryside."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Arrested for sheltering a wounded bandit; the arrest draws open disapproval — 'Who wouldn't have done the same thing?' — and marks the point at which the town turns cool toward Lewis."@en ;
    schema:name "De Micco" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/de-micco/> .

person:evans a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Allied colleague" ;
    schema:description "A member of Lewis's Field Security section."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "One of the four men left to police Naples itself; he and Lewis are sent to ransack the silk-lined apartment of Prince Pignatelli, arrested for espionage."@en ;
    schema:name "Evans" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/evans/> .

person:fortuna a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Camorrista / bandit" ;
    schema:description "A well-connected penicillin racketeer, a lieutenant of Vito Genovese."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Lewis visits him in Poggio Reale and finds him serenely certain of acquittal despite the evidence — and he is proved right: an emblem of how the powerful stay 'beyond the reach of the law' while petty thieves rot in the cells."@en ;
    schema:name "Fortuna" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/fortuna/> .

person:george-hankin a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Allied colleague" ;
    schema:description "The one member of the section, Lewis reckons, with 'deep religious scruples'."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Lands the delicate job of escorting a female internee — too important to be named in writing — to Rome; predictably, she spends the night in prison."@en ;
    schema:name "George Hankin" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/george-hankin/> .

person:giuseppina a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Notable civilian" ;
    schema:description "A 'begrimed and bedraggled' twelve-year-old orphan in Benevento who lives 'under a house' by the river."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The first abandoned girl Lewis has seen among the city's hundreds of boy-orphans; she comes matter-of-factly to the office to collect her weekly blanket — a currency worth a factory-worker's wage."@en ;
    schema:name "Giuseppina" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/giuseppina/> .

person:ingeniere-crespi a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Civilian friend" ;
    schema:description "A prosperous Naples engineer, a contact since the Anzio-leak affair."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Hosts the family mushroom- and bird-hunting expedition to the Lago di Patria that gives the book its portrait of the Neapolitan ottobrate outing."@en ;
    schema:name "Ingeniere Crespi" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/ingeniere-crespi/> .

person:ingeniere-losurdo a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Informant / contact" ;
    schema:description "An impoverished Naples engineer living in genteel poverty on the Via Chiaia."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "One of the trio — with Mosca and Lattarullo — of unmarried, well-connected, near-destitute contacts so alike they 'could easily have been members of the same family'."@en ;
    schema:name "Ingeniere Losurdo" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/ingeniere-losurdo/> .

person:luisa a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Aristocrat" ;
    schema:description "The sister of the informant Prince A."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "On the visit when her brother asks whether she might be admitted to a (nonexistent) British army brothel, she takes the refusal with the same 'polite calm' — a small scene of the era's blunt calculus of sex and survival."@en ;
    schema:name "Luisa" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/luisa/> .

person:neil-armstrong a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Allied colleague" ;
    schema:description "A Field Security colleague Lewis last saw in Tunisia eighteen months before."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Their September 1944 reunion in Benevento — Armstrong now so wary that Lewis half-expects him to grope for his testicles at the sight of a stranger — becomes the book's measure of how completely the south changes a man."@en ;
    schema:name "Neil Armstrong" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/neil-armstrong/> .

person:norah-gemelli a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Civilian friend" ;
    schema:description "A Naples neighbour of the arrested Signora Esposito-Lau, with an Irish mother and perfect English; she reads Dante and Leopardi aloud."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The centre of the circle of friends Lewis makes and treasures — and, at a dinner, the source of the true Anzio-landing leak he is then ordered to chase down, at the cost of their trust."@en ;
    schema:name "Norah Gemelli" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/norah-gemelli/> .

person:prince-a a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Aristocrat" ;
    schema:description "The head of a leading Neapolitan noble family, an absentee landlord and enthusiastic informant."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Calls at HQ with his sister Luisa to ask, with complete composure, whether she might enter an army brothel."@en ;
    schema:name "Prince A." ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/prince-a/> .

person:robert-parkinson a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Allied colleague" ;
    schema:description "A section member — 'that investigatory tiger' — who handles Lewis's doctor contacts."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Works for weeks with Placella to shut down the Pace Hospital's certificate racket; when Lewis is manoeuvred off a marriage vetting, it is Parkinson who is sent to do it instead."@en ;
    schema:name "Robert Parkinson" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/robert-parkinson/> .

person:salvatore-loreto a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Notable civilian" ;
    schema:description "A notorious infiltrator and saboteur with the Italian 10th Flotilla MAS."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Lewis finds him dying in the PW ward at Cancello, shot clean through the body; mistaken for his brother, Loreto throws his arms round Lewis's neck and rambles about their shared childhood as he dies."@en ;
    schema:name "Salvatore Loreto" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/salvatore-loreto/> .

person:sauro a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Notable civilian" ;
    schema:description "A young soldier — Italian father, German mother — behind the October 1943 delayed-mine hoax that emptied Naples."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Sent to decide whether he should be shot as a spy, Lewis finds not a 'gigantic pale-eyed Teuton' but a small, hungry boy, and reports him mentally unbalanced to save his life."@en ;
    schema:name "Sauro" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/sauro/> .

person:signora-gentile a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Civilian friend" ;
    schema:description "A Naples woman recently released from the Filangieri gaol, where the partisans had held her on 'vague charges of collaboration'."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Hosts the escapist, nostalgically frivolous dinner — Lewis's first in a civilian house — at which he meets Lola."@en ;
    schema:name "Signora Gentile" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/signora-gentile/> .

person:the-marchesa a schema:Person ;
    schema:additionalType "Aristocrat" ;
    schema:description "The last of one of the great landowning families near Benevento — 'laden with jewellery, yellowed with fever', and smoking a pipe."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Living in her ruined castle among pigeon droppings — selling pigeons to the hotel, growing vegetables in soil spread on the banqueting-hall floor — she boasts of her family's feudal oppressions and turns up, unabashed, at Lewis's supper for the Benevento elite."@en ;
    schema:name "The Marchesa" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/people/the-marchesa/> .

place:acerra a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:description "Acerra is a city and comune (municipality) in the Metropolitan City of Naples in the region of Campania, southern Italy, about 15 kilometres northeast of the capital in Naples. It has 58,585 inhabitants."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "One of the ring of towns north of Naples put under Lewis's security charge (with Casoria, Afragola and Aversa), all of them, he discovers, inside Camorra territory. He calls on its skeleton Carabinieri force and vets a local girl hoping to marry a British guardsman."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.95 ;
            schema:longitude 14.36666667 ] ;
    schema:name "Acerra" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acerra> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/acerra/> .

place:albanella a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:description "Albanella is a town and municipality in the province of Salerno in the Campania region of south-western Italy. It is 51 kilometres (32 mi) from the city of Salerno."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The rail station just south of the Sele where Fifth Army HQ — and the parasitically attached section — sits during the battle for the beachhead; German tanks push toward it to within a mile of Lewis's slit trench."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.48333333 ;
            schema:longitude 15.11666667 ] ;
    schema:name "Albanella" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albanella> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/albanella/> .

place:calabritto-palace a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Landmark" ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The palace directly across from the section's headquarters windows; Lewis watches its courtyard life at close range and, on his last day in Naples, takes a long farewell look at its huge guardian statues."@en ;
    schema:name "Calabritto Palace" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/calabritto-palace/> .

place:cancello a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "A village near which five Moroccan soldiers are lured into a house and poisoned in revenge for atrocities; also the field hospital where the captured saboteur Salvatore Loreto turns up."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.9943228 ;
            schema:longitude 14.4192148 ] ;
    schema:name "Cancello" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/cancello/> .

place:caserta a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:description "Caserta is the capital of the province of Caserta in the Campania region of Italy. An important agricultural, commercial, and industrial comune and city, Caserta is located 36 kilometres north of Naples on the edge of the Campanian plain at the foot of the Campanian Subapennine mountain range. The city is best known for the 18th-century Bourbon Royal Palace of Caserta."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Everyone visits it for its royal palace, but for Lewis it mainly marks distance: the road to it runs right past Afragola, yet Afragola's vendetta world stays, for Neapolitans, a thousand miles away. It is also a target for the Lupo band's train hijackings."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 41.06666667 ;
            schema:longitude 14.33333333 ] ;
    schema:name "Caserta" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caserta> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/caserta/> .

place:castel-capuano a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Landmark" ;
    schema:description "Castel Capuano is a castle in Naples, southern Italy. It takes its name from the fact that it was at that point in the city walls where the road led out to the city of Capua. The castle is at the southwest end of via dei Tribunali, and until recently housed the Naples Hall of Justice, which has now moved to the new Civic Center, the Centro Direzionale."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The old castle housing Naples's courts, where Lewis watches wartime justice at its most arbitrary — smuggling trials collapsing through incompetence, punishment landing only on the weak."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.85326944 ;
            schema:longitude 14.26415833 ] ;
    schema:name "Castel Capuano" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castel_Capuano> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/castel-capuano/> .

place:filangieri-prison a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Landmark" ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The Naples women's prison the section is repeatedly ordered to deliver women to on flimsy or political charges — a German-born wife, a woman the partisans had jailed — with frantic last-minute collections of warm clothing for those sent into its cold."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.836 ;
            schema:longitude 14.24 ] ;
    schema:name "Filangieri prison" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/filangieri-prison/> .

place:frattamaggiore a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:description "Frattamaggiore is a comune in the Metropolitan City of Naples, Campania, Italy. It is located 15 km (9.3 mi) north of Naples and 15 km (9.3 mi) southwest of Caserta. It was awarded the title of \"City of art\" in 2008 and named Benedictine city in 1997."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "A Camorra-country town near Aversa — home of the bandit Domenico 'Lupo', and the site of one of the harsh new joint police raids on a bandit hideout that Lewis observes."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.93333333 ;
            schema:longitude 14.28333333 ] ;
    schema:name "Frattamaggiore" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frattamaggiore> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/frattamaggiore/> .

place:monte-vergine a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Religious site" ;
    schema:description "Sanctuary of Montevergine is a Marian church in Mercogliano, located in the hamlet of Montevergine. It's a part of the Territorial Abbey of Montevergine, one of the six Italian territorial abbeys. Inside, the painting of the Madonna di Montevergine is venerated and it is estimated that about one and a half million pilgrims visit it every year."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The mountain sanctuary above Naples whose Whitsun pilgrimage is banned in 1944 to public dismay; its district also gives Lattarullo a choice tale of a millionaire woman black-marketeer."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.9362 ;
            schema:longitude 14.7285 ] ;
    schema:name "Monte Vergine" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sanctuary_of_Montevergine> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/monte-vergine/> .

place:padula a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:description "Padula is a town and comune in the province of Salerno, located in the Campania region of south-western Italy. The town is best known for the Carthusian monastery of Certosa di San Lorenzo, it is one of the largest monasteries in Italy and a notable example of Baroque architecture. The Certosa is included in the UNESCO World Heritage Site."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The former monastery used as an internment camp: the writer Curzio Malaparte has been quietly released from it, and it is where the section must collect a female internee too important to be named in writing."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.34 ;
            schema:longitude 15.65666667 ] ;
    schema:name "Padula" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Padula> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/padula/> .

place:pozzuoli a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:description "Pozzuoli is a city and comune (municipality) in the Metropolitan City of Naples in the region of Campania in southern Italy. The main city of the Phlegrean Peninsula, it has 74,665 inhabitants."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Visited on a rare day off — a town ‘strangely untouched by the war’, calmer and more foreign-feeling than the rest of the Naples area."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.84444444 ;
            schema:longitude 14.09333333 ] ;
    schema:name "Pozzuoli" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pozzuoli> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/pozzuoli/> .

place:san-sebastiano-town-near-vesuvius a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The town in the path of the 1944 lava flow, which Lewis reaches through falling ash to report on as it is threatened."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.8419 ;
            schema:longitude 14.3708 ] ;
    schema:name "San Sebastiano (town near Vesuvius)" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/san-sebastiano-town-near-vesuvius/> .

place:taranto a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:description "Taranto, historically also called Tarent in English, is a coastal city in Apulia, Southern Italy. It is the capital of the province of Taranto, serving as an important commercial port as well as the main base of the Italian navy. With a population of 185,909 as of 2025, Taranto is the second-largest city in Apulia."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The eastern port: the place Lattarullo's imaginary 'uncle from Rome' pointedly never comes from, and, at the end, the port where Lewis embarks on the ship taking him away from Naples for good."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.47111111 ;
            schema:longitude 17.24305556 ] ;
    schema:name "Taranto" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taranto> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/taranto/> .

place:torre-annunziata a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:description "Torre Annunziata is a comune (municipality) in the Metropolitan City of Naples, in the Italian region of Campania. It is located on the Gulf of Naples, at the foot of Mount Vesuvius."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "A grim coastal town south of Naples where thirty-five operational maps for the Anzio landing are found on a warehouse floor, being carried off by civilians for firewood."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.75 ;
            schema:longitude 14.45 ] ;
    schema:name "Torre Annunziata" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torre_Annunziata> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/torre-annunziata/> .

place:torrito a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "A village near Aversa ruled 'with a rod of iron' by the corrupt Marshal Benvenuto, a recurring name in unit complaints; Lewis also goes there to hear the partisan leader Albano's account of an SS reprisal massacre that has left the whole village in mourning."@en ;
    schema:name "Torrito" ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/torrito/> .

place:via-chiaia a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Street" ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Once the resort of the town's aristocracy, now lined with vast dark half-empty palazzi; Lewis's impoverished-noble contacts Losurdo and Mosca live here, and a marriage candidate's flat nearby gives her away by the fortune in black-market soap in the kitchen."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.8361063 ;
            schema:longitude 14.2455368 ] ;
    schema:name "Via Chiaia" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Via_Chiaia> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/via-chiaia/> .

place:bari a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:description "Bari is the capital city of the Metropolitan City of Bari and of the region of Apulia, on the Adriatic Sea in southern Italy. It is one of the most important economic centres of mainland southern Italy. It is a port and university city as well as the city of Saint Nicholas."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "A rear-area town to the east, mentioned only in passing — a place a returning colleague travels through, and the kind of ordinary city that Lattarullo's fictitious funeral 'uncle' is never allowed to come from (it always has to be Rome)."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 41.12527778 ;
            schema:longitude 16.86666667 ] ;
    schema:name "Bari" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bari> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/bari/> .

place:battipaglia a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:description "Battipaglia is a town and comune in the Province of Salerno, in the Campania region of southern Italy. With around 50,000 inhabitants, it is one of the most populous municipalities in the province after Salerno itself, and the main agricultural, industrial and railway hub of the Sele Plain."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "Seen only in ruins — carpet-bombed by the Allies — as Lewis hitches back toward liberated Naples: an early measure of the destruction visited on Italian towns on mere suspicion."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.61666667 ;
            schema:longitude 14.98333333 ] ;
    schema:name "Battipaglia" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battipaglia> ;
    schema:url <https://naples44.michelepasin.org/places/battipaglia/> .

place:ischia a schema:Place ;
    schema:additionalType "Town" ;
    schema:description "Ischia is a volcanic island in the Tyrrhenian Sea. It lies at the northern end of the Gulf of Naples, about 30 kilometres from the city of Naples. It is the largest of the Phlegrean Islands."@en ;
    schema:disambiguatingDescription "The Bay island where Frazer's mistress Lola and her friend retire for the summer once the affair ends; also noted for the deep ashfall it takes during the eruption."@en ;
    schema:geo [ a schema:GeoCoordinates ;
            schema:latitude 40.731204 ;
            schema:longitude 13.895721 ] ;
    schema:name "Ischia" ;
    schema:sameAs <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ischia> ;
